  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper cites the single-direction refusal work in its related-work discussion, but its own safety results evaluate offline safe/unsafe classification of fixed agent actions, not refusal behavior or the refusal direction.",
    "gate_note": "The paper describes refusal directions as prior work and evaluates steering methods on several benchmark tasks. Its safety track asks a model to classify a supplied action as safe or unsafe in context; it does not measure whether harmful user requests are refused, remove or add a refusal direction, or test the claim's predicted effects. The reported safety-screening results are recorded as related but different construct findings under fits_no_version. No results from the other benchmark tracks test a claim version.",
    "test_reason": "Arditi et al. is cited only in related work. The safety track asks models to label fixed agent actions safe or unsafe. It never extracts, ablates or adds a harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction, and never measures refusal of user requests. The tool-use 'abstain' target means declining when no suitable tool exists, not refusing harmful requests. Nothing bears on any version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is correct. Steering gives selective, inconsistent gains across tasks and models, and prompting remains a strong baseline. Target gains can come with accuracy loss, weak follow-up and poor OOD transfer. In safety screening, aggregate F1 hides the failure to recognise safe actions in harmful-source tasks.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses several representation-steering recipes, including mean-difference CAA, for safe/unsafe action-label screening. It does not derive or ablate a harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction, apply it at every layer and position, or measure refusal behavior.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Safety-screening outcomes vary by model and steering method. The paper also reports a strong source-task effect: safe actions from harmful-source tasks are classified as unsafe at very high rates.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Contrastive steering directions built from safe/unsafe action labels change offline action-screening accuracy and unsafe F1, depending on model and method. LAT and PCA gain the most; Linear Probe and SAE match the unsteered result; ITI matches or falls below it.",
        "proposed_claim": "Representation steering can modestly improve trajectory-conditioned safe/unsafe action screening, but the effect is method- and model-dependent and rarely beats prompting.",
